var SECTIONS = [
  { n:1, from:0, to:5.4, effort:'Power hike', ec:'gold', gain:3300,
    title:'Start to Mount Werner', sub:'5.4 miles · about 3,300 feet up · ski slope and two-track',
    body:'<p>Straight up the ski mountain. The Thunderhead Hiking Trail to Heavenly Daze — a black-diamond run the manual calls "very, very steep" — then the Why Not Road, the Chisholm Trail to Four Points Lodge, and the Storm Peak Challenge two-track to the aid station. It averages about 720 feet of climb per mile, and Heavenly Daze is far steeper than the average.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>The grade goes from tough to steep to brutal, and the hardest part is getting up to the gondola.</blockquote>'+
    '<p>Grass and dirt ski slope with no shade at all, then rough two-track. The Forest Service asks runners to spread out rather than wear a single rut, so expect a wide, slow-moving line. This is not the fifty-mile route — the hundred takes the steeper line.</p>'+
    '<h4>How to run it</h4><ul>'+
    '<li><b>Hike every step.</b> You cannot bank time on a climb like this, only spend it.</li>'+
    '<li><b>Fill everything.</b> The next aid station is 12.3 miles away.</li>'+
    '<li>Crew can only reach the top of the gondola, and only if it is running.</li></ul>'+
    '<div class="callout n"><b>The 2025 field averaged 2:15:48 here</b>, with a spread from 1:14 to 2:48. That spread is almost entirely the difference between people who hiked it and people who tried to run it.</div>' },

  { n:2, from:5.4, to:17.7, effort:'Run, then descend carefully', ec:'', gain:950,
    title:'Mount Werner to Fish Creek Falls', sub:'12.3 miles · 3,420 feet down · the longest stretch with no aid',
    body:'<p><b>The first 6.3 miles</b> are the Mountain View Trail along the ridge off Mount Werner — Hogan Park meadows to the south, the Zirkel Wilderness peaks to the north. The manual calls it some of the prettiest singletrack in Colorado and it is the best running you will do all race, though it honestly has lots of steep ups and downs.</p>'+
    '<p><b>Then the turn.</b> At the junction, turn down the Fish Creek Falls Trail. This is the most repeated instruction in the whole manual.</p>'+
    '<p><b>The last six miles</b> drop into the canyon — rough, rocky, over 25 percent gradient in places, roughly 3,000 feet down. The creek runs alongside for the last few miles so you can filter. You will be passing runners coming the other way on narrow tread.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>There is a rocky section where the trail goes vague. Do not go left toward the falls. Go down.</b> The trail reappears shortly.</div>'+
    '<h4>This is where the race is decided</h4>'+
    '<p>You will be three or four hours in, fresh, on a fast descent, in the best scenery of the day, with the Hares starting to come past. Every bit of quad you spend here you do not get back — and you have to climb straight back up it, then descend 3,500 feet to the finish eighty-four miles later.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout g"><b>Run the ridge. Do not race the canyon.</b> The 2025 field averaged 8:58 a mile through here — the fastest section of the whole race. That is exactly the trap.</div>' },

  { n:3, from:17.7, to:24.3, effort:'Power hike', ec:'gold', gain:2675,
    title:'Fish Creek Falls to Long Lake', sub:'6.6 miles · 2,675 feet up · steep and technical',
    body:'<p>Straight back up what you just came down, at about 405 feet per mile. Big steps, steep narrow tread, creek crossings, two waterfalls worth a five-second look. It tops out and mellows into a flat traverse round the north shore of Long Lake into the aid station, run by Karl Meltzer.</p>'+
    '<p>In 2018 the middle of the field hit this in over 90°F. Jeff Browning, who finished third, called it the most difficult part of the course.</p>'+
    '<h4>How to run it</h4><ul>'+
    '<li><b>Hike it. All of it.</b> Nobody sensible runs this. The 2025 field averaged 18:33 a mile and the fastest man through was still only doing 16:06.</li>'+
    '<li><b>Eat here.</b> The descent will have killed your appetite and you are about to go a long way.</li>'+
    '<li>No crew and no drop bag.</li></ul>' },

  { n:4, from:24.3, to:30.1, effort:'Runnable', ec:'', gain:950,
    title:'Long Lake to Summit Lake', sub:'5.8 miles · 950 feet up · rolling dirt road',
    body:'<p>The easiest section of the race. East on the Fish Creek Falls Trail, then signs put you on the Continental Divide Road — rolling dirt at a gentle grade, past Fish Creek Reservoir, more up than down as it climbs to Summit Lake.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout n"><b>One junction:</b> veer right shortly after leaving Long Lake.</div>'+
    '<h4>How to run it</h4><ul>'+
    '<li><b>Actually run this one.</b> It is the cheapest time on the course and the only place a steady road pace pays.</li>'+
    '<li>Fully exposed high-country road. Weather arrives with no warning.</li>'+
    '<li><b>Take your whole night kit at Summit</b> whether or not it is dark yet.</li></ul>' },

  { n:5, from:30.1, to:44.5, effort:'Mixed, then run', ec:'', gain:825,
    title:'Summit Lake to Billy\'s to Dry Lake', sub:'14.4 miles · 2,044 feet down · the longest segment',
    body:'<p><b>First 4.2 miles:</b> stay on FR310 past the first intersection to where the road tees after a parking area on the left. Turn <b>left onto Buff Pass Road</b>, go just over a mile, then <b>left onto FR306</b> — the top of Flash of Gold. Past a couple of well-marked forks onto singletrack, to Billy\'s Rabbit Hole. Minimal aid, so stock up.</p>'+
    '<p><b>Then 10.2 miles of Flash of Gold.</b> Purpose-built flowing singletrack through dense aspen groves, dropping about 1,800 feet on endless mellow switchbacks. Every race recap names this the highlight of the course — most runners hit it as the sun drops and the aspens light up.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout n"><b>Two places where it merges onto half a mile of powerline two-track.</b> Be ready for the turn back onto singletrack. Cross the upper Spring Creek bridge and veer left past the BTR connector. You bottom out at a creek crossing; take the short cut-through on your right after the small bridge up to Buff Pass Road, turn left, and it is a short way to Dry Lake.</div>'+
    '<h4>How to run it</h4><ul>'+
    '<li><b>Get your light out at Summit or Billy\'s</b>, not on the trail. Darkness lands in here.</li>'+
    '<li>This is the section that produced most of the 2022 drops when a storm hit runners leaving Summit.</li>'+
    '<li>The 2025 field averaged 14:07 a mile down Flash of Gold — the second-fastest section of the race. If your legs are good, this is where you make real time.</li></ul>' },

  { n:6, from:44.5, to:51.2, effort:'Runnable', ec:'', gain:225,
    title:'Dry Lake to Olympian Hall', sub:'6.7 miles · 1,603 feet down · smooth trail, then town',
    body:'<p>The Spring Creek Trail: five and a half miles of good singletrack at a mild-to-moderate downhill grade, through ferns and big-leafed flora. Fifteen numbered bridges across Spring Creek — they count down. You may get a little wet near the bottom.</p>'+
    '<p>You come out at Spring Creek Trailhead and Amethyst Road, the point where the pacer rules loosen for everyone in the race. Cross the road, take the trail on your left through town, out at Fish Creek Falls Road and Third Street, then <b>across Lincoln</b> — volunteers press the crossing button but cannot stop traffic. A short paved stretch to Olympian Hall.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>Olympian is a trap.</b> The only indoor aid station: heated, real bathrooms, a hundred people milling about, and races quietly ending in chairs. Get in, get your gear checked, get out.</div>'+
    '<p>Mile 51.2 — about halfway, and you will not feel halfway.</p>' },

  { n:7, from:51.2, to:63.9, effort:'Hike up, run down', ec:'gold', gain:2300,
    title:'Olympian Hall to Olympian Hall', sub:'12.7 miles over Emerald Mountain · 2,300 feet up and back down · in the dark',
    body:'<p>The manual calls this leg simply Olympian Hall to Olympian Hall. It is a loop over Emerald Mountain, the hill directly above town, and it passes the Lane of Pain aid station twice.</p>'+'<p>Blackmer Drive up, and up. A fire road that climbs relentlessly and gets steeper as it goes.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>It just never feels like it flattens. It breaks the entire time.</blockquote>'+
    '<p>Left uphill onto Little Moab to the <b>Lane of Pain</b> — about three miles and 1,550 feet with pitches near 20 percent — to the steeply rolling Ridge Road aid station. Then right on Stairway to Heaven, descend, right on Blair Witch, right onto Quarry Mountain Trail back to Lane of Pain. From there Morning Gloria down to Lupine, left onto Emerald Meadows to the Stables, left past the Yurt, back to Olympian. The descent out is long machine-built bike switchbacks with steep berms that throw you off camber.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>You pass the Lane of Pain aid station twice. Check in both times.</b></div>'+
    '<h4>The single biggest time sink on the course</h4>'+
    '<p>The 2025 field averaged <b>4 hours 4 minutes</b> for this loop — more than any other section, and the spread ran from 3:11 to 5:01. Almost every race report names it the worst part of the race, not physically but mentally. It is a half-marathon loop between one and five in the morning that ends exactly where it started.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>It was the hardest part of the race for me. Somehow I was just really tired, really creaky, really cranky.</blockquote>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>Bears, particularly mothers with cubs, and mountain lions are explicitly flagged here.</b></div>' },

  { n:8, from:63.9, to:70.8, effort:'Power hike', ec:'gold', gain:1700,
    title:'Olympian Hall to Dry Lake', sub:'6.9 miles · 1,700 feet up · the reverse, at dawn',
    body:'<p>Back through town in the dark, across Lincoln at the light with your pacer steering, then five and a half miles grinding back up Spring Creek.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>Time to dig down and grind back up the hill to Dry Lake. Grind it.</blockquote>'+
    '<p>Walking out of a lit town into the mountains at night for a final thirty-five mile push is genuinely intimidating. Sunrise is 6:51 am; on schedule you get light partway up. The 2025 field averaged 2:33 for this and 22:08 a mile.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>This is where the posted cutoffs turn hostile.</b> Olympian at 7:45 am to Dry Lake at 10 am is two hours fifteen for 6.9 miles of climbing — faster than the field average. Anyone near the Olympian cutoff is finished, whatever the clock says.</div>' },

  { n:9, from:70.8, to:80.8, effort:'Power hike — the crux', ec:'brick', gain:2675,
    title:'Dry Lake to Billy\'s to Summit Lake', sub:'10 miles · 2,675 feet up · the hardest section of the race',
    body:'<p>The Ditch Trail contours above the Soda Creek drainage, then <b>right at the Grouse Trail junction at about 1.3 miles</b>, and up. Grouse is a black-diamond, experts-only <i>downhill</i> mountain bike trail — roughly 2,000 feet of climb in 5.8 miles, with rock gardens, drops, narrow steep passages and cliffside exposure. Parts cross bare granite marked only with white painted dashes and cairns. Blind corners for descending bikers, and you will be on it on a Saturday morning when the bike traffic is live.</p>'+
    '<p>At about 4.5 miles you hit Buff Pass Road: <b>left for under two-tenths of a mile</b>, then <b>left onto Flash of Gold</b>. Billy\'s is 5.8 miles from Dry Lake. Then 4.2 rolling, gradual miles up to Summit Lake.</p>'+
    '<h4>The manual is wrong about this</h4>'+
    '<p>The manual says Fish Creek is the hardest section. Finishers flatly disagree.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>I am going to assert that this is by far the hardest, most technical section of the race. This section will either make or break your race.</blockquote>'+
    '<p>The 2025 field averaged <b>4 hours 2 minutes</b> for these ten miles — 24 minutes a mile, the slowest ground on the course. Mile 70 to 81, second day, no sleep. Do not panic when it takes four and a half hours.</p>'+
    '<p>Arriving at Summit: <i>"You are up. The race is not over, but it is done."</i></p>' },

  { n:10, from:80.8, to:89.0, effort:'Hike and run', ec:'', gain:900,
    title:'Summit Lake to Long Lake', sub:'8.2 miles · the high point of the course, 10,557 feet',
    body:'<p>A different route than your outbound. Head east to the junction with the Wyoming Trail, which is also the Continental Divide National Scenic Trail.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>The access onto the Wyoming Trail is small and vague. Do not miss it.</b></div>'+
    '<p>Then south on rolling singletrack through high forest and meadow, most of it near 10,000 feet, cresting the high point around mile 86. Gone are the smooth road grades — this is a series of rolling passes. The 2025 field averaged 2:45 and 20:11 a mile.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>This section will take you eight miles, and it will feel every inch of it. I found myself wondering if they would ever take us downhill.</blockquote>'+
    '<h4>The most exposed part of the course</h4>'+
    '<p>No aid, no crew, no way out. In 2022 a pair left their rain gear in the Summit drop bag to save weight and got caught in a hailstorm at 10,500 feet with no shell. This is exactly why the 2026 mandatory gear rule exists. Carry it.</p>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>At the four-way junction with the Percy Trail, around mile 86 or 87, take the right-hand trail.</b> Left is the fifty-mile course, and Saturday afternoon fifty-milers will be moving through. Then right, west, onto the Fish Creek Falls Trail for 1.7 miles to Long Lake.</div>' },

  { n:11, from:89.0, to:95.8, effort:'Hike and run', ec:'', gain:1350,
    title:'Long Lake to Mount Werner', sub:'6.8 miles · 1,350 feet up · rolling, with one last climb',
    body:'<p>Back onto the Mountain View Trail, veering left at the junction. Three or four rolling passes up and over, then the final climb to Mount Werner. You will probably see fifty-milers, who will cheer for you.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>I would call this the longest six miles of my life. Not the hardest, not the most technical. The miles just seem to go forever.</blockquote>'+
    '<div class="callout w"><b>The posted 8 pm cutoff here is a formality.</b> You cannot make the finish from Mount Werner on the cutoff line — it would mean six downhill miles in sixty minutes at mile ninety-nine. Be through by 6:30 pm.</div>' },

  { n:12, from:95.8, to:101.8, effort:'Controlled descent', ec:'brick', gain:0,
    title:'Mount Werner to the finish', sub:'6 miles · 3,486 feet down · rough dirt road',
    body:'<p>The Storm Peak Challenge two-track down past Four Points Lodge and Rainbow Saddle, onto the Why Not access road, some singletrack, one small stream crossing, then pavement into the base area. The same finish as the fifty. No navigation required.</p>'+
    '<h4>This is the widest spread on the whole course</h4>'+
    '<p>The 2025 field averaged 1:37 for these six miles. The fastest did it in <b>49 minutes</b>. The slowest took <b>2 hours 59</b>. Nothing else on the course separates people by a factor of three. That gap is quads, and quads are decided back at mile 12 in the Fish Creek canyon.</p>'+
    '<blockquote>Six miles of steep downhill will be the final bout of torture that the course will be throwing at you.</blockquote>'+
    '<div class="callout g"><b>Poles.</b> One 2022 finisher\'s single stated regret was not putting poles in the Summit Lake drop bag for exactly this descent. Poles are legal for everyone in 2026.</div>'+
    '<p>You will probably do this in the dark. <b>The clock does not stop until you hug the Designated Hugger.</b></p>' }
];

